        "citations": null,
        "text": "I'll analyze the California statute carefully. Key observations:\n\n- §86113: Lobbyist prepares a periodic report with activity expenses and contributions, and provides it to the lobbyist employer/firm (within two weeks after each calendar quarter).\n- §86114: Lobbying firms file periodic reports (includes lobbyist's §86113 report embedded).\n- §86116: Lobbyist employers file periodic reports (includes lobbyist's §86113 report embedded).\n- §86117: Reports filed during month following each calendar quarter.\n\nThe lobbyist itself does NOT file a separate report with the Secretary of State — the lobbyist provides their report to the firm/employer, which then files it. So per the framing instructions, the \"lobbyist as named filer of a separate spending report\" framing is FALSE for California. However, §86113 does require the lobbyist to \"complete and verify a periodic report\" — there's some ambiguity. I'll treat the lobbyist's §86113 report as a required lobbyist filing (the lobbyist completes/verifies it; it gets filed via the firm/employer per §86114(a)(4) and §86116(e)).\n\nGiven the framing emphasis: \"FALSE in regimes where the statute mandates the PRINCIPAL as the filer\". The lobbying firm/lobbyist employer files — but the lobbyist's own report is a discrete required document. I'll mark the lobbyist-as-filer questions FALSE since the actual filing with Secretary of State is by the firm/employer, not the lobbyist individually.\n\nFor cadence: quarterly (§86117).",
